WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.208 --> 00:00:02.791
(upbeat music)

00:00:04.766 --> 00:00:06.760
- This is "StarTalk,"

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:08.360
and I'm your personal astrophysicist,

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:09.600
Neil deGrasse Tyson,

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.820
and we're here in a
"Cosmic Queries" edition.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:15.220
And I have a new co-host,

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:17.230
never co-hosted before,

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:18.400
Ray Ellin.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.233
Ray!

00:00:19.233 --> 00:00:20.520
- Hello, hello.
- Ray in the house.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:21.760
- Great to see you, Neil.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.610
- I know you 'cause
you're our warm-up guy,

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:27.350
the warm-up comedian from
when we do live "StarTalks."

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:28.230
- Four seasons now.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.130
- Four seasons.
- Four seasons, yeah.

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:32.240
- So now we've put you
in front of the camera.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:33.972
- Great, fantastic, I'm ready.

00:00:33.972 --> 00:00:36.130
(Neil laughs)
I'm ready for it.

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:36.963
- Can you handle it?

00:00:36.963 --> 00:00:39.227
- I think I can.
- Can you handle it?

00:00:39.227 --> 00:00:40.960
- "StarTalk" is such a fun show

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:41.860
to work on.
- Oh, thank you.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:45.105
Yeah, that's not me, I'm
thanking you for 50 us.

00:00:45.105 --> 00:00:46.640
- There's a trickle-down effect.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:48.880
It starts with you and it runs through,

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:50.200
it ripples through the entire

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:51.290
cast and crew.
- Oh excellent.

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:53.078
It's all labors of love for us.

00:00:53.078 --> 00:00:54.770
And I'm always delighted

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:57.660
you make the crowd feel
at home and comfortable,

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:59.570
you always insult them intermittently.

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:01.890
- Very sporadically.
(Neil laughs)

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:03.100
When they deserve it.
- When they deserve it.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:05.510
They deserved it when it happens.

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:08.010
Excellent, and are you
active on social media?

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:09.457
- I am.

00:01:09.457 --> 00:01:11.202
@RayComedy on Instagram.

00:01:11.202 --> 00:01:12.090
- RayComedy?

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:13.420
- On Instagram, yeah.
- Instagram, okay.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:16.536
- Ray Romano is very upset
that I got RayComedy.

00:01:16.536 --> 00:01:17.369
- Okay. (laughs)

00:01:17.369 --> 00:01:18.202
- How did you get that?

00:01:18.202 --> 00:01:20.290
How, why?
(Neil laughs)

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:21.640
I'm like, you're fine without it.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:24.283
And then Twitter is @RayEllin.

00:01:24.283 --> 00:01:25.116
- RayEllin.

00:01:25.116 --> 00:01:27.430
R-A-Y-E-L--
- L-I-N.

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:28.909
- I-N, you got it, okay dude.

00:01:28.909 --> 00:01:29.760
- Thank you.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:31.680
- So, this is "Cosmic Queries."

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:32.630
- [Ray] Love "Cosmic Queries."

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:35.100
- And so, you got the questions,

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.520
cold from our social media footprint.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:39.740
And bring them, I haven't
seen the questions.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.400
But the topic

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:43.230
solicited

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:45.520
is the new space race.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:46.940
- The new space race.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:48.380
- Yeah, not old boring space race

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:49.570
where we only went to the Moon.

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:52.970
The new space race where we're
trying to get into (laughs).

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:55.470
- That's right, the new and exciting,

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:57.210
updated space race.
- Updated space race.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:00.600
- Although this first question,
it does pertain to the Moon.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:03.310
It's from Ryan Espinosa on Facebook.

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:04.857
And Ryan wants to know, "Why do you think

00:02:04.857 --> 00:02:07.227
"we haven't gone back
to the Moon in decades?

00:02:07.227 --> 00:02:10.177
"And would there be any
good sustainable reason

00:02:10.177 --> 00:02:11.747
"other than preparing for Mars

00:02:11.747 --> 00:02:13.847
"to have a permanent
research station there,

00:02:13.847 --> 00:02:16.620
"perhaps even to launch spacecraft?"

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:17.710
- No.

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:19.990
Okay, next. (laughs)

00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:21.075
- No reason at all.

00:02:21.075 --> 00:02:22.090
What about the launching spacecraft?

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:23.210
- No, no, there could be reasons,

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:26.230
but the economics is hard to justify.

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:28.980
So you need a reason that
overrode the economics,

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:30.960
like war.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:32.107
Or if China says,

00:02:32.107 --> 00:02:34.900
"We're gonna put military
bases on the Moon,"

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:36.313
we're going back to the Moon.

00:02:36.313 --> 00:02:39.860
'Cause then we use that as
an argument to justify it.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:42.040
That's why we went to the
Moon in the first place.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.603
Russia was getting our upper hand.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.370
Here's something nobody talks about.

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:49.747
We're 'Muricans, right?

00:02:49.747 --> 00:02:51.310
- 'Muricans.
- 'Murrica.

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:54.889
Apostrophe M-U-R-R-I-C-A, 'Murrica.

00:02:54.889 --> 00:02:58.370
Our narrative of our history

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.700
is that we are pioneers in space, okay?

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:03.990
But let's look at it.

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:06.000
The Russians came up
with the rocket equation,

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.590
the equation that tells
you how much fuel you need

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:10.360
to put a payload into orbit.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:11.440
Why does that matter?

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:12.290
Because

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:15.260
you need fuel

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:18.140
to carry the fuel that hasn't burned yet

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:20.120
to put the payload in orbit.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:22.690
So for every pound you
wanna put into orbit,

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:24.400
you need much much more fuel

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:26.040
than you'd otherwise think you'd need.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:29.000
And that's why the Saturn
5 rocket is so tall

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:30.380
and the astronauts and all the payload

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:31.480
is just an upper little section.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:34.380
All the rest of that is
just controlled bomb.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:37.130
So, the rocket equation was Russian.

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:40.310
So the Russians put up the
first artificial satellite,

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:41.290
which was...

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:42.123
- Sputnik.
- Sputnik.

00:03:42.123 --> 00:03:44.240
And in fact I've got Sputnik right here.

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:46.270
I got this in Star City, Moscow.

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:47.460
I mean outside of Moscow.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:50.143
It's a very distant suburb
of Moscow where they,

00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:52.423
it's like our Houston,

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:55.160
except it's really just for space.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:56.664
- Are there any cowboys there?

00:03:56.664 --> 00:03:57.620
- (laughs) No.

00:03:57.620 --> 00:03:59.891
So, this is the
International Space Station.

00:03:59.891 --> 00:04:02.000
(Neil grunts)
Okay.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.610
And then we go the next one down.

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:06.830
This is Skylab.
- Oh cool.

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:08.173
- Yeah, which was the,

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:11.950
the Apollo-Soyuz Project,

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:14.440
we used the Skylab as a space station.

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:17.050
That's smaller than the
International Space Station.

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:18.220
I've no idea what this is.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:19.500
It looks like a flying saucer.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:20.740
Could've been some prototype thing

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:23.200
that the Russians designed.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.020
And what else do I have here?

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:27.620
This is some large satellite.

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:28.823
But we keep going.

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:35.670
And there's Sputnik in
the tiniest of the dolls.

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:37.979
- Look at that, that's a
very, very clever doll.

00:04:37.979 --> 00:04:39.640
- I don't know how this
translates to radio,

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:42.540
but I just disassembled
one of these dolls.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:43.751
What are they called?

00:04:43.751 --> 00:04:45.520
- Those are the...
- Petrushka...

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:46.810
The Russian dolls, yeah.

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:48.951
The Russian dolls, the
nesting Russian dolls,

00:04:48.951 --> 00:04:50.230
there's a Russian name for 'em.

00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:52.560
- I was thinking of
Faberge, it's not a Faberge.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:53.960
- No, no, no.
- No, not even close.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:55.950
- So the littlest of one in there,

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:57.690
normally they have heads of state

00:04:57.690 --> 00:05:01.600
or popes or prime ministers or tzars.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:02.650
These are spaceships.

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:03.483
- And if you cracked that one open,

00:05:03.483 --> 00:05:04.970
there'd be a little bottle of vodka.

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:05.960
And if you cracked that open,

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:07.682
there'd be a little piece of potato.

00:05:07.682 --> 00:05:09.900
- I'm not (laughs) I'm not showing it.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:13.530
So, Sputnik, they had the first
non-human animal in space.

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:14.680
Who was that?

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:16.400
- A chimp?
- No.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:18.459
- Non-human animal?
- It was a dog.

00:05:18.459 --> 00:05:19.470
- Was it really?
- Yes!

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:20.303
- What kind of a dog?
- Laika.

00:05:20.303 --> 00:05:21.690
It was a mutt.

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:23.010
I don't know if it was a mutt,

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:24.440
but I think it was kinda like a mutt.

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:27.965
It was a stray dog from Moscow.

00:05:27.965 --> 00:05:29.160
- How would you feel, you're a stray dog,

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:30.330
just running the streets of Moscow,

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:31.960
and next thing you know
you're up in orbit.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:34.660
- Well, they had no plans
to bring it back alive.

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:35.493
- Oh, wow.

00:05:35.493 --> 00:05:36.326
- So maybe you have second thoughts.

00:05:36.326 --> 00:05:38.910
However, I'm thinking to
myself, if I were dog,

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:40.130
I could be a stray dog

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:42.270
and die in the streets
of Moscow in the winter,

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:46.340
or be the most famous dog
since Lassie and die in space.

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:47.780
- That's not a bad option.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:50.571
Or how about just being adopted
by a family with a yard?

00:05:50.571 --> 00:05:51.700
(Neil laughs)

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:53.056
- A rescue dog.
- Yeah, a rescue dog.

00:05:53.056 --> 00:05:54.170
Wouldn't that be sweet?

00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:55.720
- There's some bitterness about the fact

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:57.330
there was no plans to bring it back alive.

00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:58.960
- And it was a Russian mutt.

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:01.970
- Yeah, I think it's most
of some kind of breed,

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:03.123
but I don't think it was,

00:06:03.123 --> 00:06:05.150
I don't remember it being a purebred.

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:06.810
Anyway, they had...

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:08.096
- It's (mumbles).

00:06:08.096 --> 00:06:10.050
- And the first human in space.

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:11.276
And that was who?

00:06:11.276 --> 00:06:12.183
- The first human ever in space?

00:06:12.183 --> 00:06:13.604
- Yes, Yuri Gagarin.

00:06:13.604 --> 00:06:15.190
- Yes, it was Yuri Gagarin.

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:18.370
- He said yes, that was a good. (laughs)

00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:20.050
You saved yourself on that one.

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:21.683
If I answer quickly it meant I knew it!

00:06:21.683 --> 00:06:22.817
(Neil and Ray laugh)

00:06:22.817 --> 00:06:24.110
- That was very Stewie
and Brian right there.

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:25.880
- That was very...

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:28.850
So they also had the first...

00:06:32.290 --> 00:06:33.240
A lot of firsts.

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:35.360
And then we landed on the Moon first

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:37.890
and then we say, "We win."

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:40.040
- [Ray] And obviously
we walked on the Moon.

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:41.150
- I'm just saying,

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:45.150
we landed on the Moon first
and we say, "We win the race,"

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:48.940
when almost every other
important achievement in space

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:50.450
was achieved by the Russians.

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:53.410
And the reality of that
progression of achievements

00:06:53.410 --> 00:06:55.163
is what spooked us.

00:06:55.163 --> 00:06:56.920
Because they were accomplishing things

00:06:56.920 --> 00:06:58.860
fasted and better than we were.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:01.240
And so then we leapfrogged it
and then we went to the Moon.

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:03.120
So that's all I'm saying.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:04.960
- So, but going back to what Ryan asked.

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:06.390
- I forgot the question.

00:07:06.390 --> 00:07:07.240
- No, it's okay.

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:10.270
But why isn't the Moon
used to launch spacecrafts?

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:12.290
- Oh okay, so.

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:13.460
Here's the problem.

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:15.150
If you're gonna leave
Earth to go to the Moon

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:16.520
to then go to Mars,

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:17.770
just go straight to Mars.

00:07:19.010 --> 00:07:21.150
You're gonna need fuel to land on the Moon

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:23.940
because you have to slow down.

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:25.540
Fuel is not just to go fast.

00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:28.920
Once you're going fast, how
are you gonna slow down?

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:31.150
In our atmosphere you
can use air breaking,

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:32.930
which is why we have heat shields.

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:34.870
Because oh we need heat shields
otherwise you'd burn up.

00:07:34.870 --> 00:07:37.250
Glad you're going through the atmosphere,

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:39.100
I don't need fuel to do it.

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:41.380
Using the friction and shockwaves

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:43.240
between the craft and the air

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:44.200
to slow it down

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:47.330
so that I don't need to use
retrorockets to slow down.

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:49.340
You're going to the Moon,
you need retrorockets

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:50.173
to slow yourself down.

00:07:50.173 --> 00:07:52.890
You have to carry fuel
that you're not using yet.

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:57.740
So no, the Moon would not
be a place to organize

00:07:57.740 --> 00:07:58.660
to then go to Mars.

00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:01.290
You wanna organize, organize in zero G,

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:03.320
in a Lagrangian point.

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:05.760
They're these points
where gravity balances

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:08.270
between the Moon and the
Earth and the planets

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:10.380
where you just put stuff
there, it just stays.

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:12.360
So you can make really big ships.

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:15.760
And you leave from zero gravity

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:17.660
and now you're not trying to climb out

00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:19.520
of what we call a gravitational well,

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:21.890
which is all the exhaust that's
coming out of our rockets

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:23.310
needs to do.
- That's interesting.

00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:25.430
You think that'll ever become a reality?

00:08:25.430 --> 00:08:27.890
- Possibly, but unless
there's a war driver,

00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:29.210
there's gotta be an economic driver.

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:31.830
Otherwise I don't see it
happening ever at all.

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:34.827
So I wrote a whole book on
this that I wanted to call

00:08:34.827 --> 00:08:36.387
"Failure to Launch:

00:08:36.387 --> 00:08:39.460
"The Dreams and Delusions
of Space Enthusiasts."

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:40.293
And the publisher said,

00:08:40.293 --> 00:08:42.657
"No, we can't have the
word failure in a title,

00:08:42.657 --> 00:08:44.260
"that's just bad."

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:45.480
But I loved the title.

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:46.397
They said, "By the way,

00:08:46.397 --> 00:08:47.970
"isn't there a movie with that title?"

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:51.087
Yes, except I'm using the title literally,

00:08:51.087 --> 00:08:52.010
"Failure to Launch."

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:55.270
That one was figurative
about failing to leave home

00:08:55.270 --> 00:08:56.600
and get married.

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:58.507
- So why didn't you just call it

00:08:58.507 --> 00:09:02.758
"Successes and Unsuccesses
in the World of Launching"?

00:09:02.758 --> 00:09:04.640
(Neil laughs)
- Unsuccesses.

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:06.597
So, I settled on the title

00:09:06.597 --> 00:09:08.460
"Space Chronicles: Facing
the Ultimate Frontier."

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:09.710
Half of that book

00:09:09.710 --> 00:09:11.460
is on why we didn't
continue to go to Mars.

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:12.440
- My takeaway from this is

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:13.730
that you've written so many books,

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:15.580
you can't remember the names
of the books you wrote.

00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:18.840
That is remarkable.
(Neil laughs)

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:20.953
I've just written so many,
I'm such a prolific writer,

00:09:20.953 --> 00:09:22.661
I don't know.

00:09:22.661 --> 00:09:23.530
That's amazing.

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:24.550
- I didn't know it came out that way.

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:26.370
- No, it didn't, that's awesome.

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:28.250
That was pretty impressive, actually.

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:30.110
- Can I give you my one
impressive book story?

00:09:30.110 --> 00:09:30.943
- Yeah.

00:09:30.943 --> 00:09:32.980
- My first time I ever met Carl Sagan.

00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:35.290
I was 17, I'm in his office,

00:09:35.290 --> 00:09:37.210
and I'm sitting across his desk.

00:09:37.210 --> 00:09:38.880
And he said, "I'm gonna give you a gift."

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:42.050
He reaches behind, does not even look,

00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:45.440
grabs a book from the
shelf, and it's his book,

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:46.273
and he signs it to me.

00:09:46.273 --> 00:09:48.467
And I said, "That is badass."

00:09:49.570 --> 00:09:51.552
Whatever you touch, it's
gonna be one of your books.

00:09:51.552 --> 00:09:52.580
(Neil laughs)

00:09:52.580 --> 00:09:53.850
- It's just a bookcase full of Sagan.

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:55.870
- And then it was some years later,

00:09:55.870 --> 00:09:57.170
after my sixth book or something,

00:09:57.170 --> 00:09:59.840
I had them lined up behind
me and a kid comes in

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:01.740
and I just reached back and I say, "Damn."

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:03.370
I'm like, that's what I'm doing.

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:05.290
- Did you reach back and
grab one of Sagan's books?

00:10:05.290 --> 00:10:07.170
- No. (laughs)

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:08.613
- How many books have you written?

00:10:09.795 --> 00:10:11.300
- 15.
- 15, that's incredible.

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:13.690
- But four or five of
them are co-authored,

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:15.151
'cause you can't know everything

00:10:15.151 --> 00:10:16.730
for everything you write about.

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:18.760
But some of them I'm more
proud of than others,

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:21.690
but it's an opus of my effort

00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:23.700
to communicate the universe to the public.

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:24.980
- That's amazing.

00:10:24.980 --> 00:10:28.550
My sister Abby just wrote her
second book called "Duped."

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:31.580
And I see firsthand up close

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:33.030
how difficult it is to write a book.

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:34.950
You've written that
many, that's incredible.

00:10:34.950 --> 00:10:38.410
- Oh yeah, it's a chapter of your life,

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:41.030
it's a piece of you in the book.

00:10:41.030 --> 00:10:41.863
And when you're done,

00:10:41.863 --> 00:10:46.043
you just gotta regrow the
organs that you spent.

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:51.210
Regrow your energy,
refuel your fuel tanks,

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:53.037
regain energy,

00:10:53.037 --> 00:10:56.910
reestablish your existence as an entity.

00:10:56.910 --> 00:10:58.380
'Cause you're putting so much of yourself

00:10:58.380 --> 00:10:59.213
in the book itself.
- Sure.

00:10:59.213 --> 00:11:02.090
This is why after every
episode of "StarTalk"

00:11:02.090 --> 00:11:03.817
when Chuck Nice and I say to Neil,

00:11:03.817 --> 00:11:05.230
"Hey, you wanna go out,
maybe have a glass of wine?"

00:11:05.230 --> 00:11:07.160
He's like, "No, I gotta go
home, I've things to do."

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:08.266
Like write another book.

00:11:08.266 --> 00:11:09.177
(Neil laughs)

00:11:09.177 --> 00:11:10.497
"See you later, drunks."

00:11:12.550 --> 00:11:14.600
- The amount of interstitial time

00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:16.890
most people spend doing nothing

00:11:16.890 --> 00:11:17.970
is extraordinary.
- You're writing.

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:20.500
- Yeah, so I'm writing,
that's exactly right.

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:21.797
- Great use of time.
- Yeah.

00:11:21.797 --> 00:11:23.634
- You know who else
spent some time writing?

00:11:23.634 --> 00:11:25.640
(Neil laughs)

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:28.107
Matt Herfield on Facebook.
- Next question.

00:11:28.107 --> 00:11:30.537
- [Ray] "Why are we
framing this as a race?

00:11:30.537 --> 00:11:34.670
"Is there an end goal that one
country could achieve first?"

00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:37.923
- Ooh, this was a
thoughtful question, oh man.

00:11:39.297 --> 00:11:40.480
- [Ray] It took them 30 seconds to write.

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:43.060
(Neil laughs)

00:11:43.060 --> 00:11:45.930
- Race implies there's a
start point and an endpoint.

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:48.030
And the race to the Moon that we won,

00:11:48.030 --> 00:11:50.470
but we didn't win the
race to space itself,

00:11:50.470 --> 00:11:52.530
Russia won that race.

00:11:52.530 --> 00:11:55.950
So many people say Mars is
the next obvious choice.

00:11:55.950 --> 00:11:57.380
You're not gonna go to Venus.

00:11:57.380 --> 00:12:00.320
It's hotter than a pizza
oven on Venus, by the way.

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:03.920
So Mars would be the next obvious target.

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:05.910
But I have a contrarian view here.

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:07.350
I don't wanna call it contrarian.

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:08.920
I have a,

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:10.890
an unorthodox view here.

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:15.110
I want to build the capacity to explore

00:12:15.950 --> 00:12:18.153
without reference to where you're going.

00:12:19.890 --> 00:12:23.323
So you have a warehouse of rockets.

00:12:24.890 --> 00:12:27.459
So what do you wanna do in space, Ray?

00:12:27.459 --> 00:12:28.737
- I wanna play baseball.

00:12:28.737 --> 00:12:29.570
- You wanna play baseball.

00:12:29.570 --> 00:12:30.690
So play baseball on the Moon.

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:32.003
- Loved to.
- Okay, so.

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:37.700
You should know that the
restaurants on the Moon,

00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:38.942
they have no atmosphere.

00:12:38.942 --> 00:12:40.640
(Neil laughs)

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:42.310
You saw that coming.
- I did.

00:12:42.310 --> 00:12:43.682
- You saw it. (laughs)

00:12:43.682 --> 00:12:44.690
- But please tell me about

00:12:44.690 --> 00:12:46.580
the Italian suppositories on the Moon.

00:12:46.580 --> 00:12:50.250
(Neil and Ray laugh)

00:12:50.250 --> 00:12:51.440
- So you wanna play baseball.

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:53.000
We might wanna do that on the Moon.

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:55.230
So you go, what rockets do you need,

00:12:55.230 --> 00:12:56.730
how many people are you
gonna take with you?

00:12:56.730 --> 00:12:59.160
That's this vessel with
these many rockets.

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:01.330
And you need to last this long

00:13:01.330 --> 00:13:05.250
so you need this food supply
and oxygen, that sort of thing.

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:08.620
And then you go to the Moon and have fun.

00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:10.870
There's scientists that might wanna study

00:13:10.870 --> 00:13:12.663
the possibility of life on Mars,

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:15.640
some other genesis on Mars.

00:13:15.640 --> 00:13:17.260
So they'll bring equipment with them

00:13:17.260 --> 00:13:19.200
and they wanna go there,
maybe they want robots.

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:20.140
Maybe they'll stay in orbit

00:13:20.140 --> 00:13:22.630
and control robots down on the surface.

00:13:22.630 --> 00:13:26.850
So what I want is everybody's creativity

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:30.570
to be empowered by our
capacity to explore space.

00:13:30.570 --> 00:13:34.260
And that way no one is saying
where the destination is.

00:13:34.260 --> 00:13:36.120
So in that sense it's actually not a race,

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:39.520
it's, let's all go play in our backyard,

00:13:39.520 --> 00:13:41.820
and that backyard is the solar system.

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:43.543
- Right, and well put.

00:13:44.900 --> 00:13:46.080
It's amazing to me.

00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:47.480
I always thought it's so interesting

00:13:47.480 --> 00:13:51.340
how everything has to be
framed as a competition.

00:13:51.340 --> 00:13:53.233
- Well, where we're human,

00:13:56.251 --> 00:13:58.780
and there are people who
rise to higher heights

00:13:58.780 --> 00:13:59.793
when they compete.

00:14:00.844 --> 00:14:03.100
When I first started
watching the Olympics,

00:14:03.100 --> 00:14:04.193
I thought to myself,

00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:10.080
why is it that you can set a
world record in the Olympics?

00:14:10.170 --> 00:14:12.363
Why didn't you set the
world record a week ago?

00:14:13.410 --> 00:14:14.670
It's you, you're the same person,

00:14:14.670 --> 00:14:16.760
how different are you
this week than last week

00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:18.660
or than a month ago?

00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:19.493
Why did you set a world?

00:14:19.493 --> 00:14:24.493
Because the competition,
the moment, the urge,

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:26.170
the pageantry,

00:14:26.170 --> 00:14:28.603
all comes together and creates a will,

00:14:30.597 --> 00:14:32.910
a force,

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:36.150
almost another force of nature
operating on your ambitions.

00:14:36.150 --> 00:14:38.090
- But it also creates a sense of pressure

00:14:38.090 --> 00:14:42.810
that maybe someone can
run the fastest time ever

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:43.860
if he didn't feel as pressured.

00:14:43.860 --> 00:14:46.030
For example, some people
can only sing in the shower.

00:14:46.030 --> 00:14:48.130
They can't sing on stage,
they're great in the shower--

00:14:48.130 --> 00:14:50.400
- Well, I sing really good in the shower,

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:52.684
and I could sing on stage
but no one would want me to.

00:14:52.684 --> 00:14:53.787
(Ray and Neil laugh)

00:14:53.787 --> 00:14:57.300
- Do you wanna do the remake
of "Jesus Christ Superstar"?

00:14:57.300 --> 00:14:59.260
- Yeah, "Jesus Christ Superstar"
is my favorite musical.

00:14:59.260 --> 00:15:01.780
And I saw it in 1971.

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:06.180
With an original cast
including Ben Vereen as Judas.

00:15:06.180 --> 00:15:07.979
- [Ray] Wow, you saw it
when you were two years old.

00:15:07.979 --> 00:15:09.183
(Neil laughs)
- Yes.

00:15:11.260 --> 00:15:12.093
But why did you?

00:15:12.093 --> 00:15:16.070
Oh, yeah, so I don't
have a voice that's good,

00:15:16.070 --> 00:15:17.240
I don't have a good singing voice

00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:18.850
other than I just sing in the shower.

00:15:18.850 --> 00:15:19.683
- Right, but there's some people

00:15:19.683 --> 00:15:21.870
who sound incredible in the shower

00:15:21.870 --> 00:15:23.770
and would be good in
front of a live audience,

00:15:23.770 --> 00:15:25.850
so I'm sure there's people that
participate in the Olympics

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:26.960
who maybe aren't,

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:29.060
maybe they could've
broken the world record

00:15:29.060 --> 00:15:30.300
just sort of on their own time.

00:15:30.300 --> 00:15:32.930
Like on a random Sunday they
get up, feel kind of relaxed--

00:15:32.930 --> 00:15:35.060
- So we need an Olympics with
no cameras and no audience.

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:37.740
- No cameras, no audience, no fans there.

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:38.573
Sure, exactly.

00:15:38.573 --> 00:15:39.800
It's called the Home Olympics.

00:15:39.800 --> 00:15:40.790
- The Home Olympics.

00:15:40.790 --> 00:15:43.163
All right, we'll see how that one plays.

00:15:44.670 --> 00:15:46.200
So yeah, competition drives us.

00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:50.270
So does the urge to gain wealth and power.

00:15:50.270 --> 00:15:52.560
So that's driven all the greatest projects

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:54.710
that humans have ever done in
the history of civilization.

00:15:54.710 --> 00:15:59.710
Greatest in terms of what
fraction of a society's

00:16:01.660 --> 00:16:05.980
intellectual, physical
and financial capital

00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:07.750
was invested in it.

00:16:07.750 --> 00:16:10.100
So when that's high,

00:16:10.100 --> 00:16:13.790
generally it's the praise of royalty,

00:16:13.790 --> 00:16:14.950
gods and royalty,

00:16:14.950 --> 00:16:17.350
it's economics, or it's war.

00:16:17.350 --> 00:16:19.430
And today you don't have praise of royalty

00:16:19.430 --> 00:16:20.940
triggering expensive projects

00:16:20.940 --> 00:16:23.930
the way we once we did in civilization.

00:16:23.930 --> 00:16:25.993
So that basically leaves war and money.

00:16:27.020 --> 00:16:27.970
I wished that were not true.

00:16:27.970 --> 00:16:30.010
I'm just simply reporting

00:16:30.010 --> 00:16:34.200
what has driven and
motivated people in the past.

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:36.020
And so yes, competition,

00:16:36.020 --> 00:16:39.670
to be the first to get the economic return

00:16:39.670 --> 00:16:41.340
or to get the high ground,

00:16:41.340 --> 00:16:43.660
if there's a military motive to it, yeah.

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:44.760
- [Ray] The Cheney effect.

00:16:44.760 --> 00:16:46.480
- Yeah, definitely.

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:47.860
All right, next question.

00:16:47.860 --> 00:16:48.850
- All right, next question is

00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:52.570
from Pedro Semedio on Facebook.

00:16:52.570 --> 00:16:56.127
Pedro asks, "Are there any
international ethics or rules

00:16:56.127 --> 00:16:58.377
"for space exploration?

00:16:58.377 --> 00:17:00.757
"Let's suppose for example than Chang'e 4

00:17:00.757 --> 00:17:02.216
"had a problem when landing."

00:17:02.216 --> 00:17:04.150
- This is the Chinese mission

00:17:04.150 --> 00:17:05.957
that landed on the back side of the Moon.

00:17:05.957 --> 00:17:07.000
- Yes.
- Yes, okay.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:08.390
- The backside or dark side?

00:17:08.390 --> 00:17:09.410
Or same thing?

00:17:09.410 --> 00:17:10.910
- No, no.

00:17:10.910 --> 00:17:11.743
- Two different things?

00:17:11.743 --> 00:17:14.877
- In spite of the 1973
album by Pink Floyd,

00:17:14.877 --> 00:17:15.868
"The Dark Side of the Moon,"

00:17:15.868 --> 00:17:16.701
there's no dark side of the Moon.

00:17:16.701 --> 00:17:18.530
All sides of the Moon receive light.

00:17:18.530 --> 00:17:19.760
Yeah, a day in the Moon lasts a month.

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:21.662
- Oh, no kidding.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:17:21.662 --> 00:17:24.040
So there's a far side to the Moon.

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:26.210
That's why I said the back side.

00:17:26.210 --> 00:17:27.750
- I got you.
- Okay.

00:17:27.750 --> 00:17:28.583
- Not the underside.

00:17:28.583 --> 00:17:30.845
- But it's not a place
where the sun don't shine.

00:17:30.845 --> 00:17:31.793
(Ray laughs)
Okay.

00:17:33.269 --> 00:17:35.637
- So, okay, so in Chang'e 4,

00:17:35.637 --> 00:17:37.307
"If it had a problem when landing

00:17:37.307 --> 00:17:39.317
"and the potato seeds and silkworms

00:17:39.317 --> 00:17:40.797
"were spread over lunar soil,

00:17:40.797 --> 00:17:42.857
"were they to be punished somehow

00:17:42.857 --> 00:17:45.730
"for contaminating lunar
soil with terrestrial life?"

00:17:45.730 --> 00:17:47.560
Two different things.

00:17:47.560 --> 00:17:49.240
- So there are three things here.

00:17:49.240 --> 00:17:50.860
In 1976, there's something called

00:17:50.860 --> 00:17:52.820
the International Space Treaty.

00:17:52.820 --> 00:17:55.470
There's a much longer title
but you wanna shorten it,

00:17:55.470 --> 00:17:57.370
International Space Treaty.

00:17:57.370 --> 00:18:00.200
Signed by more than a 100 countries.

00:18:00.200 --> 00:18:04.570
And it's a code of conduct for space.

00:18:04.570 --> 00:18:06.080
Very kumbaya.

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:10.160
They saw that the Cold
War was getting hotter

00:18:10.160 --> 00:18:15.000
and space was becoming a contested regime

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:16.510
of international politics,

00:18:16.510 --> 00:18:19.060
and the UN said, "We gotta
do something about this."

00:18:19.060 --> 00:18:21.690
So in it, it says, if you're in trouble,

00:18:21.690 --> 00:18:24.940
even if we're enemies on the
ground, I gotta help you.

00:18:24.940 --> 00:18:26.927
I will bring you food or help you.

00:18:28.530 --> 00:18:30.190
It's very--
- Unifying.

00:18:30.190 --> 00:18:31.840
- Forward-looking and unifying.

00:18:31.840 --> 00:18:32.673
It's beautiful.

00:18:32.673 --> 00:18:35.543
My cynical skepticism is,

00:18:36.470 --> 00:18:38.653
if you can actually
pull that off in space,

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:42.978
why don't you pull that off
down here on Earth? (laughs)

00:18:42.978 --> 00:18:44.820
Why is space different?

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:47.273
You're humans and we're human.

00:18:48.340 --> 00:18:50.940
We kill each other from limited resources,

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:52.930
we kill each other 'cause
your skin color is different,

00:18:52.930 --> 00:18:54.490
'cause you worship a different god,

00:18:54.490 --> 00:18:57.310
'cause you live on a different
side of a line on the sand.

00:18:57.310 --> 00:18:58.217
Why do we think,

00:18:58.217 --> 00:19:02.400
and if you can't accomplish it
up there, let's do that here?

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:04.690
Let that be evidence that
you could do it in space.

00:19:04.690 --> 00:19:06.530
That's my cynical side of it.

00:19:06.530 --> 00:19:07.777
But, so we have that.

00:19:07.777 --> 00:19:08.610
- But you're right.

00:19:08.610 --> 00:19:09.900
I mean, you are right.

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:11.530
We don't allow greed on rocket ships.

00:19:11.530 --> 00:19:13.860
- I know. (laughs)

00:19:13.860 --> 00:19:18.300
In addition, NASA has a branch of itself

00:19:18.300 --> 00:19:20.323
called Planetary Protection Office.

00:19:21.270 --> 00:19:26.270
And this protects contamination
in destination places

00:19:26.550 --> 00:19:28.133
of Earth life.

00:19:29.330 --> 00:19:31.970
And it protects Earth life from
what could be a contaminant

00:19:31.970 --> 00:19:33.930
brought back from space.

00:19:33.930 --> 00:19:35.310
And there are regulations about that

00:19:35.310 --> 00:19:39.210
and what level of
contamination and sterilization

00:19:39.210 --> 00:19:43.540
is allowed for different kinds of objects.

00:19:43.540 --> 00:19:48.540
So Europa, that one has the
highest chance of having life.

00:19:48.990 --> 00:19:52.700
So you want the most
steril objects going there

00:19:52.700 --> 00:19:54.550
to minimize the risk of contamination.

00:19:54.550 --> 00:19:57.370
The Moon itself is kind of sterile.

00:19:57.370 --> 00:20:00.550
We're not worried about
contaminating life that's there.

00:20:00.550 --> 00:20:02.040
Because it's got no atmosphere,

00:20:02.040 --> 00:20:04.720
the ultraviolet light
which is hostile to life,

00:20:04.720 --> 00:20:05.553
the molecules of life,

00:20:05.553 --> 00:20:07.120
just breaks apart the molecules.

00:20:08.840 --> 00:20:10.840
There's asteroids hitting all the time.

00:20:10.840 --> 00:20:14.530
So we're not worried that if
you go to the Moon and sneeze,

00:20:14.530 --> 00:20:16.580
you're gonna contaminate
a future experiment

00:20:16.580 --> 00:20:17.680
that we're conducting.

00:20:18.850 --> 00:20:21.830
So there's a lot written on it,

00:20:21.830 --> 00:20:23.920
but no, nobody gets punished.

00:20:23.920 --> 00:20:25.660
You look bad on an international stage

00:20:25.660 --> 00:20:27.473
but there's no punishment.

00:20:28.330 --> 00:20:29.680
It's no go back three spaces.

00:20:29.680 --> 00:20:32.380
- So you're saying China
will not be penalized

00:20:32.380 --> 00:20:36.120
for bringing 50 children on that mission

00:20:36.120 --> 00:20:37.864
so they can make sneakers on the Moon?

00:20:37.864 --> 00:20:40.280
