00:22:39.433 --> 00:22:40.967
This is--
- "Cosmic Queries."

00:22:40.967 --> 00:22:42.000
- "Cosmic Queries."

00:22:42.000 --> 00:22:44.713
This is from Woody from
Adelaide, Australia.

00:22:46.377 --> 00:22:50.407
"What is the goal line for
scoring in the new space race?

00:22:50.407 --> 00:22:52.157
"Last time it was boots on the Moon.

00:22:52.157 --> 00:22:55.237
"A badass station on Mars would be cool."

00:22:56.250 --> 00:22:57.940
- That's a great question.

00:22:57.940 --> 00:22:59.860
I can't speak for everyone,

00:22:59.860 --> 00:23:01.560
but I can definitely list certain things

00:23:01.560 --> 00:23:04.187
that have not been accomplished yet.

00:23:04.187 --> 00:23:08.150
So, if you go to the
Moon and set up a colony,

00:23:08.150 --> 00:23:09.581
whether it's a permanent colony

00:23:09.581 --> 00:23:13.083
or a place that you visit seasonally,

00:23:14.090 --> 00:23:15.570
you wanna be able to

00:23:16.650 --> 00:23:20.830
use local resources to make stuff.

00:23:20.830 --> 00:23:24.063
And in NASA abbreviation lingo it's ISRU.

00:23:25.700 --> 00:23:28.093
In situ resource utilization.

00:23:28.930 --> 00:23:31.630
So it's better discussed
with regards to Mars.

00:23:31.630 --> 00:23:35.940
You go to Mars, are you
bringing all the water with you

00:23:35.940 --> 00:23:37.670
that you'll ever need?

00:23:37.670 --> 00:23:39.240
No, that's kinda stupid.

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:40.810
If you're driving to Los Angeles,

00:23:40.810 --> 00:23:42.380
you don't bring all the water you need.

00:23:42.380 --> 00:23:45.230
You're gonna stop places
and pick up some water.

00:23:45.230 --> 00:23:47.070
And you refuel your tank.

00:23:47.070 --> 00:23:49.750
You're not gonna go with one fuel tank.

00:23:49.750 --> 00:23:50.583
It's gotta be bigger

00:23:50.583 --> 00:23:51.860
'cause you gotta make it to Los Angeles.

00:23:51.860 --> 00:23:54.123
No, there are filling
stations along the way.

00:23:55.080 --> 00:23:58.623
So one goal is that space becomes,

00:23:59.960 --> 00:24:04.290
space itself becomes a
self-supporting commodity,

00:24:04.290 --> 00:24:05.900
if you will.

00:24:05.900 --> 00:24:07.620
So you don't need a huge rocket,

00:24:07.620 --> 00:24:10.730
you need a half-sized rocket
and you refuel halfway there.

00:24:10.730 --> 00:24:12.740
You don't need all the water
you're ever gonna drink.

00:24:12.740 --> 00:24:16.080
Get to Mars, sift the water
out from beneath the soil,

00:24:16.080 --> 00:24:19.040
beneath the surface,

00:24:19.040 --> 00:24:21.340
melt that and now you have water.

00:24:21.340 --> 00:24:24.980
Oh, by the way, water also
serves as rocket fuel.

00:24:24.980 --> 00:24:27.970
Build a factory, separate the
two molecules, the two atoms.

00:24:27.970 --> 00:24:29.120
What are they in water?

00:24:30.260 --> 00:24:31.650
What's the molecular form of water?

00:24:31.650 --> 00:24:32.630
- Hydrogen and

00:24:34.850 --> 00:24:36.040
oxygen.
- Thank you.

00:24:36.040 --> 00:24:37.140
H2O.

00:24:37.140 --> 00:24:37.973
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:24:37.973 --> 00:24:38.806
Yeah, H2O.

00:24:38.806 --> 00:24:39.820
- Sometimes when you ask me a question,

00:24:39.820 --> 00:24:40.870
I think you're trying to trick me.

00:24:40.870 --> 00:24:41.848
- No!

00:24:41.848 --> 00:24:43.577
I'm not a trick question guy, no.

00:24:43.577 --> 00:24:45.440
- Not a trickster.
- No.

00:24:45.440 --> 00:24:46.830
I ask very simple questions.

00:24:46.830 --> 00:24:48.056
- Well, thank goodness for that.

00:24:48.056 --> 00:24:48.889
- (laughs) Okay.

00:24:48.889 --> 00:24:52.660
So, H2O, if you separate those two atoms,

00:24:52.660 --> 00:24:54.860
and put them in separate liquified tanks.

00:24:54.860 --> 00:24:56.480
So liquified hydrogen, liquified.

00:24:56.480 --> 00:24:57.390
Very cold.

00:24:57.390 --> 00:24:59.350
You cool it down, it liquefies.

00:24:59.350 --> 00:25:01.570
And then have a nozzle that connects them,

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:05.083
it's a highly exothermic reaction.

00:25:06.190 --> 00:25:07.023
There's endothermic,

00:25:07.023 --> 00:25:09.300
means the reaction
absorbs energy around it.

00:25:09.300 --> 00:25:11.320
Exothermic means it releases energy.

00:25:11.320 --> 00:25:14.000
You bring hydrogen oxygen
together, it's rocket fuel.

00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:15.860
So you can have water
there that you can drink,

00:25:15.860 --> 00:25:17.963
water that can serve as a,

00:25:18.890 --> 00:25:21.150
as rocket fuel,

00:25:21.150 --> 00:25:22.600
so then you don't have to bring it.

00:25:22.600 --> 00:25:24.630
Okay, well, how about food?

00:25:24.630 --> 00:25:28.110
Well, Mark Watney solved that one.

00:25:28.110 --> 00:25:29.560
He grew potatoes.

00:25:29.560 --> 00:25:31.280
You gotta go with something first.

00:25:31.280 --> 00:25:33.230
Figure that out, whether
it's gonna be green houses,

00:25:33.230 --> 00:25:34.063
this sort of thing.

00:25:34.063 --> 00:25:37.420
So one of the goal lines
or high bars for us is

00:25:37.420 --> 00:25:39.520
in situ resource utilization.

00:25:39.520 --> 00:25:41.250
And now that we have 3D printers,

00:25:41.250 --> 00:25:42.083
if something breaks,

00:25:42.083 --> 00:25:45.003
I need a knot, a bolt,
a fan blade, print it.

00:25:46.150 --> 00:25:47.184
Send in the CAD.

00:25:47.184 --> 00:25:49.420
Load up the CAD from Earth.

00:25:49.420 --> 00:25:50.750
The computer-assisted design.

00:25:50.750 --> 00:25:52.020
Load it from Earth.

00:25:52.020 --> 00:25:52.853
'Cause I'm not gonna design it on Mars,

00:25:52.853 --> 00:25:54.510
I don't have that talent.

00:25:54.510 --> 00:25:55.590
Get somebody to design it.

00:25:55.590 --> 00:25:57.290
Oh, here's that new part you need.

00:25:58.364 --> 00:26:00.410
- Or just bring the designer with you.

00:26:00.410 --> 00:26:02.660
- No, they wouldn't have to.

00:26:02.660 --> 00:26:03.530
- You just bring the machine.

00:26:03.530 --> 00:26:05.260
- Yeah, there's a time
delay of like 20 minutes

00:26:05.260 --> 00:26:06.130
but that's fine.

00:26:06.130 --> 00:26:09.350
- What about long
distance energy transfer?

00:26:09.350 --> 00:26:12.490
Is that something that we're sort of?

00:26:12.490 --> 00:26:15.010
- You mean, oh well, no, our rockets,

00:26:15.010 --> 00:26:17.100
we're still using chemical
fuels in our rockets.

00:26:17.100 --> 00:26:18.490
You know what we need?

00:26:18.490 --> 00:26:19.550
Warp drives.

00:26:19.550 --> 00:26:21.240
(Neil and Ray laugh)

00:26:21.240 --> 00:26:22.430
Warp drives.

00:26:22.430 --> 00:26:23.560
- We need phasers and warp drives.

00:26:23.560 --> 00:26:25.270
- Phasers and warp drives.

00:26:25.270 --> 00:26:27.230
And we don't have flying cars yet

00:26:27.230 --> 00:26:29.010
but we need the warp
drives like real soon.

00:26:29.010 --> 00:26:31.500
Then you can cross the galaxy
during the TV commercial.

00:26:31.500 --> 00:26:33.450
Otherwise you're dead.

00:26:33.450 --> 00:26:38.230
The time it takes to travel
star to star is incommensurate

00:26:38.230 --> 00:26:40.763
with the life expectancy
of human physiology.

00:26:41.720 --> 00:26:42.960
- Is that right?
- Yeah.

00:26:42.960 --> 00:26:45.930
So going the fastest we've
ever gone, sent anything,

00:26:45.930 --> 00:26:48.910
if I aim for Alpha Century,
the nearest star to the Sun,

00:26:48.910 --> 00:26:50.068
ask me how long it takes?

00:26:50.068 --> 00:26:51.230
- How long does it take?

00:26:51.230 --> 00:26:53.090
