00:32:13.410 --> 00:32:17.283
This is from Almatastar from Instagram.

00:32:18.347 --> 00:32:21.977
"When will the Space Force
start cleaning up space,

00:32:21.977 --> 00:32:22.977
"or is there another program

00:32:22.977 --> 00:32:25.936
"that's already working on space cleaning?

00:32:25.936 --> 00:32:28.027
"Saludos de Puerto Rico."

00:32:28.027 --> 00:32:29.400
Greetings from Puerto Rico.

00:32:29.400 --> 00:32:32.200
- Space Force.

00:32:32.200 --> 00:32:33.850
So a lot of talk about Space Force

00:32:33.850 --> 00:32:36.600
'cause Trump said he wants a Space Force.

00:32:36.600 --> 00:32:38.300
But let me just say unequivocally

00:32:39.220 --> 00:32:42.870
that just because Trump mentioned it

00:32:42.870 --> 00:32:44.470
does not mean it's a crazy idea.

00:32:45.310 --> 00:32:47.870
Just for the Trump naysayers out there.

00:32:47.870 --> 00:32:49.460
Just want, just--

00:32:49.460 --> 00:32:50.840
- The moment they hear that word Trump,

00:32:50.840 --> 00:32:51.780
they immediately just.

00:32:51.780 --> 00:32:53.030
- Yeah, they can't listen to anything

00:32:53.030 --> 00:32:54.030
that comes out of his mouth.

00:32:54.030 --> 00:32:55.899
Space Force is...

00:32:55.899 --> 00:32:57.090
- You think it's practical?

00:32:57.090 --> 00:32:57.960
Reasonable?

00:32:57.960 --> 00:33:00.580
- I don't care, really,

00:33:00.580 --> 00:33:03.490
but I can tell you the benefits of it.

00:33:03.490 --> 00:33:08.180
It will focus attention that
is already allocated to space

00:33:08.180 --> 00:33:10.260
within the Air Force.

00:33:10.260 --> 00:33:12.620
The Air Force has the
United States Space Command.

00:33:12.620 --> 00:33:14.220
The Air Force has been
thinking about space

00:33:14.220 --> 00:33:15.627
ever since we've had access to space.

00:33:15.627 --> 00:33:18.300
And you know who else
is thinking about space?

00:33:18.300 --> 00:33:23.000
The CIA and other reconnaissance agencies.

00:33:23.000 --> 00:33:25.493
Because space is the high ground.

00:33:26.560 --> 00:33:29.280
So when you think of Space Force

00:33:29.280 --> 00:33:31.590
and you wanna think of space war,

00:33:31.590 --> 00:33:35.226
the urge is "Star Wars" with
lasers and weapons and phasers.

00:33:35.226 --> 00:33:36.059
(Neil laughs)
- So, phasers.

00:33:36.059 --> 00:33:38.155
- You need the photon torpedoes.

00:33:38.155 --> 00:33:42.340
But it turns out it's very impractical

00:33:42.340 --> 00:33:45.080
to attack Earth targets from space,

00:33:45.080 --> 00:33:47.630
'cause you're moving 18,000
miles an hour in orbit

00:33:48.570 --> 00:33:50.250
and that's just really fast.

00:33:50.250 --> 00:33:52.110
To then aim and do something on Earth?

00:33:52.110 --> 00:33:53.020
It's just.

00:33:53.020 --> 00:33:56.290
When we can send an
intercontinental ballistic missile

00:33:56.290 --> 00:34:01.290
and hit a target any place
on Earth within 45 minutes,

00:34:03.060 --> 00:34:06.300
to say, oh we gotta wait until
the satellite is in position.

00:34:06.300 --> 00:34:07.650
No, no, you just throw it.

00:34:07.650 --> 00:34:10.490
So we already know how to kill each other

00:34:10.490 --> 00:34:13.110
from surface-based weaponry.

00:34:13.110 --> 00:34:16.463
We already know that, so
space is not improving that.

00:34:17.320 --> 00:34:18.854
Quote improving that.

00:34:18.854 --> 00:34:22.120
So, space is a place for reconnaissance.

00:34:22.120 --> 00:34:25.500
And by the way, the Air Force,

00:34:25.500 --> 00:34:27.950
no one questions why do
we have an Air Force.

00:34:27.950 --> 00:34:29.690
They used to be a branch of the Army.

00:34:29.690 --> 00:34:30.680
It was the Army Air Force

00:34:30.680 --> 00:34:31.887
throughout the whole Second World War.

00:34:31.887 --> 00:34:33.890
- But the notion of Space
Force to be sort of--

00:34:33.890 --> 00:34:34.960
- But let me (mumbles)!

00:34:34.960 --> 00:34:37.230
- Oh, my bad, my bad!
- Telling you!

00:34:37.230 --> 00:34:38.470
- Yeah, I got you.

00:34:38.470 --> 00:34:40.140
- The engineer that fixes the tank,

00:34:40.140 --> 00:34:41.080
is that the same engineer

00:34:41.080 --> 00:34:43.020
that fixes the airfoil on an airplane?

00:34:43.020 --> 00:34:44.340
No.

00:34:44.340 --> 00:34:46.740
The grunt on the ground
who's carrying the weapon,

00:34:46.740 --> 00:34:49.010
is that the same soldier training

00:34:49.010 --> 00:34:51.330
as the pilot of the jet fighter?

00:34:51.330 --> 00:34:52.250
No.

00:34:52.250 --> 00:34:56.260
So you branch off Air Force from the Army.

00:34:56.260 --> 00:34:58.990
And no one is really questioning that,

00:34:58.990 --> 00:34:59.993
the value of that.

00:35:00.970 --> 00:35:05.190
So to branch off Space
Force from the Air Force,

00:35:05.190 --> 00:35:07.050
why are you gonna have an issue with that?

00:35:07.050 --> 00:35:08.793
And, by the way, if you're gonna do that,

00:35:10.874 --> 00:35:13.410
let's add priorities

00:35:13.410 --> 00:35:15.340
to whatever it is they were doing before.

00:35:15.340 --> 00:35:18.380
And one of them would
be let's clean up space.

00:35:18.380 --> 00:35:20.390
Space debris.
- Space litter.

00:35:20.390 --> 00:35:22.050
- Oh, it's litter.

00:35:22.050 --> 00:35:24.220
I think that's why we haven't
been visited by aliens.

00:35:24.220 --> 00:35:25.053
- Too many balloons up there?

00:35:25.053 --> 00:35:26.587
- No, no, they just looked at it and said,

00:35:26.587 --> 00:35:28.477
"You guys are nasty."

00:35:28.477 --> 00:35:30.037
- "You're slobs."
- "You're just slobs."

00:35:30.037 --> 00:35:32.820
- "You dirty filthy people."
- "You dirty, filthy."

00:35:32.820 --> 00:35:34.610
So NASA tracks space debris

00:35:34.610 --> 00:35:35.930
so that when it launch the next thing,

00:35:35.930 --> 00:35:38.270
they don't slam into it.

00:35:38.270 --> 00:35:42.200
The Space Station has
limited maneuverability power

00:35:42.200 --> 00:35:44.330
in case something is headed its way.

00:35:44.330 --> 00:35:46.368
So, yeah.

00:35:46.368 --> 00:35:47.800
Another one of what I'd want them to do

00:35:47.800 --> 00:35:50.430
is deflect the asteroids.

00:35:50.430 --> 00:35:52.520
I don't wanna be like the
dinosaurs, going extinct.

00:35:52.520 --> 00:35:55.940
'Cause we have a space program
and the dinosaurs didn't.

00:35:55.940 --> 00:35:57.470
Dinosaurs, if they had a space program,

00:35:57.470 --> 00:35:58.820
no, they didn't.
- I didn't know that.

00:35:58.820 --> 00:36:00.850
- I'm sorry I didn't tell you
that before the show. (laughs)

00:36:00.850 --> 00:36:03.350
- I thought the pterodactyls had a--

00:36:03.350 --> 00:36:04.833
- Oh, they would lead, (laughs)

00:36:04.833 --> 00:36:06.208
they're the flying ones, right?

00:36:06.208 --> 00:36:08.160
- Yeah, they're leading the Space Force.

00:36:08.160 --> 00:36:09.060
- Yeah.

00:36:09.060 --> 00:36:12.240
- They're the front man.
- They're the front man.

00:36:12.240 --> 00:36:16.740
Plus, the badass one was Rodan from,

00:36:16.740 --> 00:36:18.730
the supersonic pterodactyl.

00:36:18.730 --> 00:36:20.430
- Supersonic.
- Yeah, definitely.

00:36:21.410 --> 00:36:23.990
So, what am I saying?

00:36:23.990 --> 00:36:27.540
Yeah, I think you'd want
to add to their portfolio.

00:36:27.540 --> 00:36:31.100
- But wouldn't Space Force sort of be,

00:36:31.100 --> 00:36:34.430
because it's all of space, outer space,

00:36:34.430 --> 00:36:37.040
wouldn't that need to be an
international organization?

00:36:37.040 --> 00:36:39.610
- You know, that's a
really good, that's good.

00:36:39.610 --> 00:36:43.580
That's like saying the air
molecules above this country

00:36:43.580 --> 00:36:44.750
belong to the United States.

00:36:44.750 --> 00:36:45.583
- Correct.

00:36:45.583 --> 00:36:47.650
- That's kind of stupid, no.
- It's insane.

00:36:47.650 --> 00:36:49.450
- The air moves, it's insane, right.

00:36:50.350 --> 00:36:52.600
You can say, I don't want
you over this country,

00:36:52.600 --> 00:36:54.760
but to declare ownership of air,

00:36:54.760 --> 00:36:56.870
when air travels around the world?

00:36:56.870 --> 00:36:58.397
Yeah, it's that kinda thing.

00:36:58.397 --> 00:36:59.360
"I own space."

00:36:59.360 --> 00:37:00.210
No, you don't.

00:37:00.210 --> 00:37:01.250
You can't own space.

00:37:01.250 --> 00:37:02.410
- I'd like to dispute air rights

00:37:02.410 --> 00:37:03.575
on top of my apartment building.

00:37:03.575 --> 00:37:05.090
(Neil laughs)

00:37:05.090 --> 00:37:07.310
(mumbles) the atmosphere
all over the world.

00:37:07.310 --> 00:37:09.720
- So yeah, ownership is a thing.

00:37:09.720 --> 00:37:12.340
So I think that any
discussion about ownership

00:37:12.340 --> 00:37:15.380
is about who owns the
Moon, who owns an asteroid.

00:37:15.380 --> 00:37:17.590
You go to an asteroid to
mine its natural resources,

00:37:17.590 --> 00:37:18.423
do you own it?

00:37:20.071 --> 00:37:20.904
Or who owns it?

00:37:20.904 --> 00:37:22.083
- Finder's keepers, man.

00:37:22.083 --> 00:37:24.310
(Neil laughs)

00:37:24.310 --> 00:37:25.600
No, but it's a great point.

00:37:25.600 --> 00:37:27.960
That's like saying, do we own the Moon?

00:37:27.960 --> 00:37:31.755
We put a flag on the
Moon, the Moon's ours.

00:37:31.755 --> 00:37:32.588
It's US light.

00:37:32.588 --> 00:37:35.301
- Yeah, we didn't put
the UN flag on the Moon.

00:37:35.301 --> 00:37:38.210
- We put the US flag so it's US light.

00:37:38.210 --> 00:37:41.980
It's the United States, it's
our, you know what I'm saying.

00:37:41.980 --> 00:37:43.770
Little island, sort of speak.

00:37:43.770 --> 00:37:46.070
And by the way, you cannot see the flag,

00:37:46.070 --> 00:37:49.940
the flag that Neil
Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin

00:37:49.940 --> 00:37:51.100
planted on the Moon,

00:37:51.100 --> 00:37:53.190
you cannot see that with any,

00:37:53.190 --> 00:37:55.010
the Hubble Telescope can't capture that?

00:37:55.010 --> 00:37:59.190
- It's too small to resolve
with ground-based telescopes

00:37:59.190 --> 00:38:01.040
or the size of telescopes
that are in orbit.

00:38:01.040 --> 00:38:02.050
But you can see the flag

00:38:02.050 --> 00:38:04.300
from satellites that orbit the Moon.

00:38:04.300 --> 00:38:06.760
So we have imagining, you
can see the Rover tracks,

00:38:06.760 --> 00:38:07.593
it's kinda cool.

00:38:07.593 --> 00:38:08.800
- Oh, that's cool, that's awesome.

00:38:08.800 --> 00:38:12.033
- You know what they got
wrong in, what movie was that?

00:38:13.690 --> 00:38:14.910
Was it--
- "Interstellar"?

00:38:14.910 --> 00:38:15.743
- No, no.

00:38:15.743 --> 00:38:17.047
A "Transformers."

00:38:17.890 --> 00:38:19.970
I think it was one of the
"Transformers" movies.

00:38:19.970 --> 00:38:23.790
They show one of their
craft passing by the Moon

00:38:23.790 --> 00:38:28.140
and you see the landing spot
for the Apollo astronauts

00:38:28.140 --> 00:38:30.350
but it still has the limb on it.

00:38:31.383 --> 00:38:32.326
- [Ray] Oh, is that right?

00:38:32.326 --> 00:38:33.951
- Yeah, 'cause that departed from it,

00:38:33.951 --> 00:38:37.230
it just has the spidery leg at the bottom.

00:38:37.230 --> 00:38:38.320
Yeah, so they left the whole thing

00:38:38.320 --> 00:38:40.430
'cause you wouldn't
recognize it as anything

00:38:40.430 --> 00:38:41.693
if it's just a platform.

00:38:42.740 --> 00:38:44.253
- Did you retweet that at some point?

00:38:44.253 --> 00:38:46.160
- No, it was a little obscure.

00:38:46.160 --> 00:38:47.430
- Well, you have too many
"Game of Thrones" tweets

00:38:47.430 --> 00:38:48.980
to get through, so you know.
(Neil laughs)

00:38:48.980 --> 00:38:50.990
- Yeah, it just looks so naked

00:38:50.990 --> 00:38:53.710
to just have the base and these four legs,

00:38:53.710 --> 00:38:56.250
so they left the whole module there

00:38:56.250 --> 00:38:58.250
that the astronauts occupied

00:38:58.250 --> 00:39:00.670
just so it looks like a landing spot.

00:39:00.670 --> 00:39:04.530
- Is it understood that
if China were to land

00:39:04.530 --> 00:39:07.810
right where we have
landed, the US had landed,

00:39:07.810 --> 00:39:10.490
that you're not supposed
to tamper with any of that?

00:39:10.490 --> 00:39:11.370
The flag, the--

00:39:11.370 --> 00:39:14.330
- When has the history of human conduct

00:39:14.330 --> 00:39:16.440
fulfilled anything that was
supposed to be anything?

00:39:16.440 --> 00:39:17.870
- Yeah.

00:39:17.870 --> 00:39:20.663
- Yeah, if they went up
and like broke the flag.

00:39:21.710 --> 00:39:22.690
- Technical foul!

00:39:22.690 --> 00:39:25.460
(Neil laughs)

00:39:25.460 --> 00:39:26.583
- 15 yard penalty.

00:39:27.620 --> 00:39:29.720
So, yeah.

00:39:29.720 --> 00:39:32.230
That would be, I don't wanna
say it was an act of war,

00:39:32.230 --> 00:39:35.300
I would say it would be a
profound act of disrespect

00:39:35.300 --> 00:39:37.423
and there would probably be repercussions.

00:39:37.423 --> 00:39:39.680
- [Ray] Certainly with
this administration.

00:39:39.680 --> 00:39:40.663
- Disrespect.

00:39:41.540 --> 00:39:43.912
Specially since--
- There'd be a Moon tariff.

00:39:43.912 --> 00:39:46.400
- (laughs) Specially since,

00:39:46.400 --> 00:39:48.920
even though we planted an American flag,

00:39:48.920 --> 00:39:53.237
Neil Armstrong laid a plaque that said,

00:39:53.237 --> 00:39:55.887
"We come in peace

00:39:55.887 --> 00:39:57.137
"for all mankind."

00:39:58.130 --> 00:40:01.817
It didn't say, "We come for
America, for Americans."

00:40:03.600 --> 00:40:07.690
And I don't know any act of exploration

00:40:07.690 --> 00:40:09.660
where upon planting a flag,

00:40:09.660 --> 00:40:11.437
the next words uttered were,

00:40:11.437 --> 00:40:14.950
"We come in peace for
everyone, all of humanity."

00:40:14.950 --> 00:40:16.100
The history of that exercise,

00:40:16.100 --> 00:40:18.170
specially Europeans leaving Europe,

00:40:18.170 --> 00:40:21.683
is, "I put a flag here, now we own it.

00:40:22.677 --> 00:40:26.277
"And we have warships to
back up that statement."

00:40:27.260 --> 00:40:30.320
No matter who you find living there.

00:40:30.320 --> 00:40:34.130
So it was a fundamentally different act.

00:40:34.130 --> 00:40:36.140
And so we'll see what
the future of this is.

00:40:36.140 --> 00:40:37.183
I don't know.

00:40:38.667 --> 00:40:39.830
Got time for a couple more,
give me a couple more.

00:40:39.830 --> 00:40:41.317
- Alexis Wardrole, Instagram.

