You're ready?

00:41:10.070 --> 00:41:12.110
Which world leader should
we send into space?

00:41:12.110 --> 00:41:13.540
Okay, so, first,

00:41:13.540 --> 00:41:16.420
we should send all the
flat-earthers into space.

00:41:16.420 --> 00:41:18.150
- [Ray] Okay. (laughs)

00:41:18.150 --> 00:41:19.950
- I think they wanted that anyway.

00:41:19.950 --> 00:41:22.330
- So, Kyrie Irving, not a
world leader but he can go.

00:41:22.330 --> 00:41:24.204
- Just so they can orbit the Earth

00:41:24.204 --> 00:41:26.510
and see that Earth is a sphere.

00:41:26.510 --> 00:41:28.380
And then you bring them back
and say, "You happy now?"

00:41:28.380 --> 00:41:29.310
- Oh, for educational needs.

00:41:29.310 --> 00:41:31.440
- Yeah, I'm an educator, dude.
- That's true.

00:41:31.440 --> 00:41:32.514
- I'm not trying to get rid of 'em,

00:41:32.514 --> 00:41:33.454
I trying to help 'em out.

00:41:33.454 --> 00:41:34.661
- Get rid of them.
- No.

00:41:34.661 --> 00:41:36.930
- One of the dumb ideas
you have, get out of here!

00:41:36.930 --> 00:41:40.700
- You demonstrate the roundness of Earth.

00:41:40.700 --> 00:41:41.840
Okay.

00:41:41.840 --> 00:41:46.840
Next, you send all world leaders.

00:41:48.080 --> 00:41:51.930
It'll be like a PeopleMover van.

00:41:51.930 --> 00:41:52.830
No, it'd have to be bigger.

00:41:52.830 --> 00:41:55.290
How many world, there are
hundreds of world leaders.

00:41:55.290 --> 00:41:56.610
There's hundreds, okay.

00:41:56.610 --> 00:41:57.443
You ready?

00:41:57.443 --> 00:41:58.340
- [Ray] Thousands if
you count the islands.

00:41:58.340 --> 00:41:59.900
- This is a quote.

00:41:59.900 --> 00:42:00.733
Exactly.

00:42:01.980 --> 00:42:04.360
But leaders of established nations

00:42:04.360 --> 00:42:05.440
is a hundred and something.

00:42:05.440 --> 00:42:07.250
How many countries are there?

00:42:07.250 --> 00:42:08.630
- [Ray] I think it's 174?

00:42:08.630 --> 00:42:10.730
- Yeah, it's between
one and 200 countries.

00:42:10.730 --> 00:42:12.650
We've got our crack team
researching this now.

00:42:12.650 --> 00:42:13.483
- 195.
- How much?

00:42:13.483 --> 00:42:14.607
- 195.
- Yeah, 195.

00:42:14.607 --> 00:42:16.870
So 200 countries, so 200 world leaders.

00:42:16.870 --> 00:42:17.773
- Okay.
- Okay, here we go.

00:42:17.773 --> 00:42:19.652
- Do they get a plus one?

00:42:19.652 --> 00:42:22.377
- (laughs) Okay, 400 world leaders.

00:42:22.377 --> 00:42:23.720
(Ray laughs)

00:42:23.720 --> 00:42:25.490
I'm gonna read a quote
from Edgar Mitchell.

00:42:25.490 --> 00:42:26.323
- Okay.

00:42:26.323 --> 00:42:28.020
- Apollo?

00:42:28.020 --> 00:42:28.977
- Three.

00:42:28.977 --> 00:42:31.010
(Neil laughs)

00:42:31.010 --> 00:42:32.880
- Apollo 14.
- 14.

00:42:32.880 --> 00:42:34.610
- Apollo 1 through 6 were scrapped

00:42:34.610 --> 00:42:37.393
after we lost the three
Apollo 1 astronauts.

00:42:37.393 --> 00:42:38.880
So the next Apollo was Apollo 7.

00:42:38.880 --> 00:42:40.025
- That's my question again.

00:42:40.025 --> 00:42:41.310
(Neil laughs)

00:42:41.310 --> 00:42:42.627
- Okay, so, here we go.

00:42:42.627 --> 00:42:45.747
"You develop an instant
global consciousness,

00:42:45.747 --> 00:42:47.407
"a people orientation,

00:42:47.407 --> 00:42:51.077
"an intense dissatisfaction
with the state of the world,

00:42:51.077 --> 00:42:54.747
"and a compulsion to
do something about it.

00:42:54.747 --> 00:42:56.627
"From out there on the Moon,

00:42:56.627 --> 00:43:00.247
"international politics look so petty.

00:43:00.247 --> 00:43:02.997
"You want to grab a politician
by the scruff of the neck

00:43:02.997 --> 00:43:06.611
"and drag him a 1/4
million miles out and say,

00:43:06.611 --> 00:43:09.690
"'Look at that, you son of a bitch.'"

00:43:09.690 --> 00:43:11.030
- That's great.

00:43:11.030 --> 00:43:12.000
- Ooh.

00:43:12.000 --> 00:43:14.810
- That's juicy.
- Juicy.

00:43:14.810 --> 00:43:19.040
Just give a cosmic
perspective reality check

00:43:19.040 --> 00:43:20.630
to every politician

00:43:20.630 --> 00:43:22.920
and then bring them back down to Earth.

00:43:22.920 --> 00:43:24.270
I think they'll be changed.

00:43:25.180 --> 00:43:27.650
That's what the universe can do to you.

00:43:27.650 --> 00:43:32.650
When you see Earth as only
nature can present it to you,

00:43:33.880 --> 00:43:37.973
with oceans and land and clouds,

00:43:39.000 --> 00:43:41.673
no color-coded national boundaries,

00:43:43.314 --> 00:43:45.710
none of the usual trappings

00:43:45.710 --> 00:43:49.600
of who owns what part of
what section of the world,

00:43:49.600 --> 00:43:50.633
it's just a planet.

00:43:51.710 --> 00:43:52.993
Our life-giving planet.

00:43:55.086 --> 00:43:56.410
If you can't make peace on that planet

00:43:56.410 --> 00:43:57.883
with fellow human beings,

00:44:00.920 --> 00:44:04.890
you don't deserve the power
that science has given you

00:44:06.670 --> 00:44:08.530
to serve as a shepherd

00:44:08.530 --> 00:44:10.743
of our future survival on this planet.

00:44:11.940 --> 00:44:12.773
You don't deserve it.

00:44:12.773 --> 00:44:15.840
You should just go somewhere else.

00:44:15.840 --> 00:44:19.810
Put people in power who
understand what Earth is,

00:44:19.810 --> 00:44:22.280
what it means to us and
all other life on Earth

00:44:22.280 --> 00:44:25.870
and what steps they
need to take to cherish

00:44:25.870 --> 00:44:28.953
this place that we have
borrowed from our descendants.

00:44:30.051 --> 00:44:33.810
- I think that would be a
great unifying experience,

00:44:33.810 --> 00:44:34.643
I think.

00:44:34.643 --> 00:44:35.476
If you could do that.

00:44:35.476 --> 00:44:38.200
If you could send those
200 people up together.

00:44:38.200 --> 00:44:39.560
I really think it would.

00:44:39.560 --> 00:44:41.086
- After you send out the flat-earthers.

00:44:41.086 --> 00:44:41.960
(Neil laughs)
- Yeah.

00:44:41.960 --> 00:44:43.955
I actually would like
to become a flat-earther

00:44:43.955 --> 00:44:45.560
just so I can get that trip.
- Just to get the first trip.

00:44:45.560 --> 00:44:46.393
- That's a nice trip.

00:44:46.393 --> 00:44:48.970
It's far better than getting
a freebie to Cape Cod.

00:44:48.970 --> 00:44:51.690
- So Ray, we gotta wrap, we
gotta call it quits here.

00:44:51.690 --> 00:44:54.060
- Well, before we go I
just want everyone to know

00:44:54.060 --> 00:44:57.353
I came across Neil's
book, "Accessory to War."

00:44:57.353 --> 00:44:58.330
- Well, you're in my office
so the book is there.

00:44:58.330 --> 00:44:59.873
- Well, that's how I came across it.

00:44:59.873 --> 00:45:01.610
I was snooping around his office

00:45:01.610 --> 00:45:04.420
and in between all the bobbleheads of him

00:45:04.420 --> 00:45:08.760
and all the "Star Wars" Campbell Soups

00:45:08.760 --> 00:45:10.880
that he has up there on the shelf.

00:45:10.880 --> 00:45:14.309
- But this one is Primordial Soup.

00:45:14.309 --> 00:45:15.930
Let's get your soup straight here.

00:45:15.930 --> 00:45:16.860
- Wait, what?

00:45:16.860 --> 00:45:19.430
- Primordial Soup.
(Ray laughs)

00:45:19.430 --> 00:45:20.970
- On that self over there you have a--

00:45:20.970 --> 00:45:23.820
- Oh those are "Star Wars"
Campbell Soups, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:45:23.820 --> 00:45:26.257
- But this is "Accessory to
War: The Unspoken Alliance

00:45:26.257 --> 00:45:29.424
"Between Astrophysics and the Military."

00:45:29.424 --> 00:45:31.100
This is a book you wrote a few months ago?

00:45:31.100 --> 00:45:33.030
- It came out, yeah, last September.

00:45:33.030 --> 00:45:34.960
I co-author it with Avis Lang.

00:45:34.960 --> 00:45:37.210
It's like 500 pages.

00:45:37.210 --> 00:45:38.370
So my book before that

00:45:38.370 --> 00:45:40.520
was "Astrophysics for People in a Hurry."

00:45:40.520 --> 00:45:42.427
This is astrophysics for
people not in a hurry.

00:45:42.427 --> 00:45:44.202
Just make that clear.

00:45:44.202 --> 00:45:47.240
- The other book, I read on a subway ride.

00:45:47.240 --> 00:45:49.620
This one's gonna get me a
subway ride with four stops.

00:45:49.620 --> 00:45:51.530
It's gonna take me a
little more extra time.

00:45:51.530 --> 00:45:52.363
But, again--

00:45:52.363 --> 00:45:53.196
- But thanks for the
plug, thanks for the plug.

00:45:53.196 --> 00:45:55.213
It's another labor of love.

00:45:55.213 --> 00:45:59.710
It's the history, 'cause
science is not untouched by war,

00:45:59.710 --> 00:46:02.410
and the founding forward that enable it,

00:46:02.410 --> 00:46:04.450
that empower it, that feed it.

00:46:04.450 --> 00:46:07.890
And so this is just a
candid review and assessment

00:46:07.890 --> 00:46:10.200
of what that relationship has been

00:46:10.200 --> 00:46:13.060
between the history of
astrophysics and war itself.

00:46:13.060 --> 00:46:14.410
- I'm excited to try to tackle this.

00:46:14.410 --> 00:46:17.093
You are the most prolific
non-comedian I've ever met.

00:46:17.093 --> 00:46:17.960
(Neil laughs)

00:46:17.960 --> 00:46:21.270
There's some comics I know
who just write endlessly.

00:46:21.270 --> 00:46:23.010
And of all the non-comics,
this is unbelievable.

00:46:23.010 --> 00:46:24.530
15 books.

00:46:24.530 --> 00:46:26.010
- There's a new word
for being a non-comic,

00:46:26.010 --> 00:46:28.050
it's called non-comic. (laughs)

00:46:28.050 --> 00:46:29.650
- A civilian.
- A civilian.

00:46:29.650 --> 00:46:30.483
We gotta go.

00:46:30.483 --> 00:46:32.380
Ray, thanks for doing this!
- Thanks man.

00:46:32.380 --> 00:46:33.213
A lot of fun.

00:46:33.213 --> 00:46:34.290
- Okay, we're gonna get you back again.

00:46:34.290 --> 00:46:35.123
- Love it.

00:46:35.123 --> 00:46:36.880
- And the invitation to Aruba stands.

00:46:36.880 --> 00:46:38.660
- Wide open.
- All right.

00:46:38.660 --> 00:46:41.750
You've been listening to,
possibly even watching,

00:46:41.750 --> 00:46:45.270
this space race episode of "StarTalk."

00:46:45.270 --> 00:46:47.550
And I've been your host,
Neil deGrasse Tyson.

00:46:47.550 --> 00:46:50.592
And as always, I bid
you to keep looking up.

00:46:50.592 --> 00:46:53.175
(upbeat music)

